  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through Thursday)\nIssued at 1247 PM CST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nWith continued northerly winds Monday, CAA behind Sunday's \nPacific cold front prevents highs from rising above 60F north of \nthe Rio Grande basin, with highs remaining in the upper 40s F for \nhigher elevations and low-lying areas of the SE NM plains and \nnortheast Permian Basin. Temperatures will feel more typical of \nlate December than mid-November, while lows will continue to \nretain the more familiar early November-like \"mildness\" of above \nfreezing albeit chilly temperatures in the 40s to lower 50s F. \nMid-level ridging over the Southern Great Plains will subtly build\nback in next week, while an overall longwave troughing pattern \npersists over the southern US, especially for the Pacific SW into \nDesert SW. This keeps temperatures below average through the week,\nwith highs remaining in the 50s and 60s F, 40s F higher \nelevations Tuesday, and only warming back up into the lower to mid\n60s F from Wednesday through Thursday. Low (less than 30%) rain \nchances are possible with a weak Pacific cold front mid-week next \nweek, but at this time, no major impacts (whether related to rain,\nwind, or dust) seem apparent with any weather systems next week. \nHighs in the mid to upper 60s to 70s F may only return by next \nFriday given the mid to upper air pattern, while lows continue to \nfall into the 40s to lower 50s F as the dry autumn air maintains a\n25F-30F diurnal range.",
